WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 54: The Peshtigo Fire of 1871: The Forgotten Hurricane of Flame

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.840
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.200
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:13.280
[Rebecca] On the night of October 8, 1871, a devastating tragedy struck the Upper Midwest.

4
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:26.400
[Rebecca] While history textbooks remember that date for the Great Chicago Fire, a far deadlier and more terrifying catastrophe erupted 250 miles to the north in the dense pine forests of northeastern Wisconsin.

5
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:36.080
[Rebecca] The Peshtago Fire remains the deadliest wildfire in recorded American history, claiming an estimated 1200 lives in a matter of hours.

6
00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.920
[Rebecca] George tells the harrowing story of the hurricane of flame.

7
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:15.920
[George] The entire community was constructed of wood, wooden homes, wooden sidewalks, sawdust-paved streets, and mountains of dry timber stacked along the riverbanks.

8
00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:22.960
[George] The surrounding forests of Marinette and Ocanto counties were thick with virgin white pine, maple, and hemlock.

9
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:29.360
[George] But that autumn, an unprecedented drought had turned the entire North Woods into a virtual powder keg.

10
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:41.040
[George] Creeks had dried to cracked mud, wells were pumped dry, and stagnant sulphurous smoke hung over the valleys for weeks as railroad clearing crews burned brush along the newly surveyed tracks.

11
00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:52.880
[George] Thousands of French Canadian, Belgian, and German immigrants had settled around Peshtago, clearing small farmsteads in the heavy timber and working in the noisy, bustling woodenware mills.

12
00:01:52.880 --> 00:02:03.800
[George] Sawdust piles 30 feet high flanked the river, releasing pungent clouds of resinous pine dust that coated every windowpane and rooftop in the bustling manufacturing community.

13
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:14.360
[George] Lumber schooners tied up at the docks loaded millions of bored feet of cured lumber, their sails furled as captains waited for the persistent haze of smoke to clear from Green Bay.

14
00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:21.080
[George] On Sunday evening, October 8, an extraordinary meteorological event ignited the disaster.

15
00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:28.840
[George] A massive low-pressure cyclonic front swept eastward across the Great Plains, colliding with the dry air over Wisconsin.

16
00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:34.360
[George] As the barometer plummeted, gale force winds began howling through the forest canopy.

17
00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:42.760
[George] At around 9 o'clock, the disparate logging fires scattered across millions of acres suddenly merged into a single monstrous firestorm.

18
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:50.840
[George] The immense updraft generated winds estimated at over 110 miles per hour, creating a true fire hurricane.

19
00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:57.240
[George] Pine trees three feet in diameter were snapped like matchsticks and sucked skyward into the thermal vortex.

20
00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:08.200
[George] The heat became so intense that sand melted into liquid glass, railroad car wheels fused to their iron rails, and chimneys exploded from internal thermal expansion.

21
00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:16.280
[George] A terrifying dull roar, likened by survivors to the scream of a thousand locomotives, announced the arrival of the fire.

22
00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:26.200
[George] The cyclonic front generated thermal winds that reached tornado strength, lifting burning roofs and throwing flaming wooden wagons through the night air like toys.

23
00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:36.680
[George] The sound of the approaching firestorm was described by survivors as a deafening mechanical scream, shaking the earth like a subterranean earthquake minutes before the flames appeared.

24
00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:46.640
[George] The barometer dropped precipitously as the cyclonic storm approached, setting cattle lowing in the pastures and causing household pets to claw desperately at cabin doors.

25
00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:53.600
[George] Witnesses in Peshtago looked to the southwest and saw what appeared to be a black wall topped by a crimson aurora.

26
00:03:53.600 --> 00:04:00.960
[George] Within ten minutes, chimney high fireballs, carried on thermal currents, began raining down directly onto the town.

27
00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:08.080
[George] Houses ignited simultaneously on both sides of the river before the main fire front even touched the village limits.

28
00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:16.880
[George] Father Peter Pernin, the Catholic parish priest, stepped onto his porch and saw the sky raining glowing embers as large as fence posts.

29
00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:22.480
[George] The church bell began to toll wildly in the gale until the bell tower itself caught fire.

30
00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.640
[George] Panic seized the streets.

31
00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:37.680
[George] Families rushed into the smoke-choked avenues in their nightclothes, carrying infants and small belongings, only to find the wooden sidewalks beneath their feet bursting into bright blue flame from the intense radiant heat.

32
00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:46.480
[George] Citizens running through the streets found the sawdust roads exploding beneath their boots, forcing them to abandon their belongings and sprint toward the river.

33
00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:56.880
[George] In the town center, men loaded handcarts with family heirlooms and bank ledgers, only to watch them incinerate instantly in mid-street from radiant thermal energy.

34
00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:06.880
[George] Father Pernin carried the holy sacrament in a small metal monstrance through the showering sparks, praying aloud with parishioners who knelt in terror upon the wooden sidewalks.

35
00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:17.360
[George] The galeforce thermal winds snapped giant white pines like matchsticks, hurling 60 foot trunks across the river and smashing wooden storehouses in the center of town.

36
00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:28.880
[George] With roads in every direction blocked by fallen, burning timber, Father Pernin and hundreds of fleeing citizens realized that the freezing waters of the Peshtago River were their only sanctuary.

37
00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:35.880
[George] They plunged down the steep mud banks and waded out into the forty-foot-wide river, submerging themselves to their necks.

38
00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:39.480
[George] The scene in the water was one of nightmare and despair.

39
00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:48.680
[George] Hundreds of men, women and children were packed together, fighting off cattle, horses and wild hogs that were also swimming frantically in the current.

40
00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:53.400
[George] The air above the river was filled with superheated gas and flying embers.

41
00:05:53.400 --> 00:06:01.080
[George] Survivors repeatedly dipped wet woolen blankets over their heads, surfacing only for seconds at a time to gasp for air.

42
00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:05.720
[George] Those who stood upright had their hair and clothing ignited by the searing heat.

43
00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:10.440
[George] Those who lost their footing in the dark current were swept downstream and drowned.

44
00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:21.480
[George] In the icy waters of the Peshtago River, human beings and wild beasts stood shivering together in the current, united in a desperate struggle to draw breath beneath wet blankets.

45
00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:31.400
[George] The water of the river was so cold that hypothermia threatened to claim those who survived the heat, forcing families to huddle closely in the current to share body warmth.

46
00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:43.320
[George] Even in the freezing river, flying timbers and burning roofs rained down from above, forcing survivors to repeatedly submerge themselves completely beneath the dark water to avoid fiery impact.

47
00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:55.400
[George] While hundreds managed to reach the river in Peshtago, the farming settlements in the surrounding wilderness, known as the Upper, Middle, and Lower Sugar Bush, suffered almost total annihilation.

48
00:06:55.400 --> 00:07:00.600
[George] In these isolated clearings, pioneer families had no deep river for protection.

49
00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:07.640
[George] Many farmers dug root cellars or crawled into water wells, hoping the earth would insulate them from the inferno.

50
00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:17.040
[George] Tragically, the firestorm consumed all oxygen from the air, suffocating entire families in their shelters without a single mark of burn upon their bodies.

51
00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:26.800
[George] Other families clustered in the center of ploughed potato fields, only to be overwhelmed by rolling waves of flame that swept across the dirt like liquid gasoline.

52
00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:33.360
[George] In the lower sugar bush, not a single inhabitant survived along an entire four-mile stretch of road.

53
00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:42.880
[George] Entire farming settlements in the sugar bush clearings were wiped off the map, with nothing remaining but white ash and the melted iron bands of wagon wheels.

54
00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:54.800
[George] Searchers in the weeks following the tragedy found untouched pockets of green trees surrounded by miles of total desolation, illustrating the capricious, erratic nature of the fire tornadoes.

55
00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:07.040
[George] Medical teams arriving by steam tug from Green Bay found hundreds of survivors suffering from severe ophthalmia and skin burns, requiring weeks of emergency treatment in makeshift field tents.

56
00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:18.720
[George] Pioneer farmsteads across the sugar bush were obliterated without a trace, leaving only melted cast iron stove frames and blackened field stones to mark where thriving family homes had stood.

57
00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:28.880
[George] When morning broke on Monday, October 9, the roaring winds died away, leaving a silent, smoking wasteland shrouded in dense sulphurous fog.

58
00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:38.160
[George] Survivors staggered out of the freezing river, shivering violently and suffering from severe hypothermia, blistering burns, and smoke blindness.

59
00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:41.600
[George] The prosperous town of Peshtago was completely erased.

60
00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:46.080
[George] Every home, mill, warehouse, and bridge was reduced to white ash.

61
00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:55.680
[George] More than 800 people had perished in Peshtago alone, with another 400 dead in the surrounding settlements and across Green Bay on the Dore Peninsula.

62
00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:00.440
[George] Total fatalities were estimated between 1,200 and 1,500 souls.

63
00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:11.560
[George] The dead were laid out in long trenches in what is now the Peshtago Fire Cemetery, where many could only be identified by personal trinkets or pocket watches found in the ashes.

64
00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:23.240
[George] Governor Fairchild mobilized relief trains loaded with flour, blankets and medical supplies, directing aid to both the survivors of Chicago and the desolate forests of Peshtago.

65
00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:33.800
[George] Contributions and relief supplies arrived from across Europe and Canada, helping the resilient survivors establish new brick farmsteads and modern fire resistant mills.

66
00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:44.200
[George] In the cruelest twist of history, the Peshtago tragedy was largely overshadowed in national newspapers by the Great Chicago Fire, which occurred on the exact same evening.

67
00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:57.800
[George] While Chicago lost roughly 300 lives and had direct telegraph lines to New York, Peshtigo had its telegraph poles burned to cinders, and news of the tragedy took days to reach Governor Lucius Fairchild in Madison.

68
00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:06.760
[George] Despite being forgotten by the wider nation, the Peshtago disaster transformed forest management and civil engineering across North America.

69
00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:16.120
[George] It inspired the development of modern firebreaks, organized state conservation corps, and strict laws governing slash burning in logging camps.

70
00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:27.400
[George] Today, the Peshtago Fire Museum honors the resilience of the pioneers who rebuilt from the ashes, ensuring that the victims of America's deadliest firestorm will never be forgotten.

71
00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:39.240
[George] Modern fire science has confirmed that the extreme weather conditions of October 8th created an unprecedented convective chimney that remains a classic case study in atmospheric thermodynamics.

72
00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:47.360
[Rebecca] The Peshtigo Fire remains a solemn reminder of nature's awesome fury and the courage of those who rebuilt from the ashes.

73
00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:48.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

74
00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:53.840
[Rebecca] Join us next week for another compelling chapter of Most Fascinating Stories.

